WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:04.059
Abraham: When designers create a
new system, they often start by

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:07.870
looking at what the system needs
to do and what it should look like.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:12.670
But when designing any system, it
is always important to consider

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:16.999
cyber security as an integral part
of the design process in order to

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.990
create a system that is robust.

00:00:19.449 --> 00:00:23.040
Nicole: Traditionally organisations
have been more reactive to cyber

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.160
security, but this can be a costly
process in more ways than one.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:32.850
By being proactive and designing systems
with cyber security already built in they

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:37.419
are limiting the opportunity for tech
savvy hackers to create maximum damage.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.690
Abraham: If the specifications of
a system highlight cyber security

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:46.370
as integral, then the system will
be designed around that criterion.

00:00:46.929 --> 00:00:50.499
Nicole: Before a system is designed it
should be looked at carefully to see

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.050
what data, connections, people, and
other systems may be needed for it to

00:00:55.050 --> 00:01:00.290
operate and how these need to be taken
into consideration regarding security.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:05.149
The point at which any cyber attack could
happen should be thoroughly reviewed.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:09.550
This should be at every point at which
data is stored, manipulated, or rendered.

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:14.309
Abraham: It is also important to
assess what risks cannot be accepted

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.990
and what the consequences of an
attack would be at every level.

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.790
Companies should always do a risk
analysis beforehand to assess where the

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:26.390
potential problems might lie and what the
consequences might be if cyber security

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.250
is not implemented from the beginning.

00:01:28.789 --> 00:01:32.320
Nicole: Everyone involved in the
design process should also consider

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.820
the cost of not doing something
versus the cost of doing it.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:40.639
So, what would happen when a system
stops functioning due to a cyber-attack.

00:01:41.139 --> 00:01:45.420
It can greatly impact both users
of the system and the organisation

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.420
who owns and manages the system.

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:51.480
Abraham: Unauthorised access may
mean that the system has been

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:57.000
modified or had its data destroyed,
meaning that if a ‘backup’ is not

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.580
available all that data may be lost.

00:02:00.309 --> 00:02:04.929
Also, all that data accessed, could
be sold on, or given to others to use.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.210
Nicole: You only have to look at the
impact this has had on companies and

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:13.510
organisations such as Eurofins scientific
who were infected by ransomware

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.119
and the NHS which was brought to a
standstill by the WannaCry outbreak.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.170
For more information on
this check out our weblinks.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.680
Abraham: Costs don’t just involve
repairing or patching a system

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:28.480
or payment of ‘ransoms’ to get a
system back online or retrieve data.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:33.720
They involved much greater costs
such as being fined under GDPR or NIS

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.089
legislation as well as general business
costs, and major reputational damage

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.149
let alone the chance of litigation.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:44.380
There are also issues related to
loss of trust amongst customers,

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:49.209
users or suppliers and an inability
to meet contractual obligations.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:53.249
Nicole: So, if the worst happens
and a system is found vulnerable,

00:02:53.559 --> 00:02:54.999
what are the consequences?

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.899
If data is imported from an external
or less trusted source it may have

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.820
been designed to attack a system.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.390
Employees at all levels should be made
aware of the consequences of a data

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.209
breach and their own responsibility
as part of the organisation.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:14.970
Abraham: It is always important that
structure data can be validated or

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.120
else it should be transformed, or
it needs to be rendered carefully.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:23.160
The complexity of malicious code
in documents means that it is

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.990
safer to transform the file or
content into another format.

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:32.139
Nicole: Checking that the structure
and content of data is as expected goes

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:36.940
some way to ensuring that malicious code
will not be injected into a system, but

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.609
this only works in simple file formats.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:44.390
If validation and transformation are
not possible rendering information in

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:48.730
a disposable environment not linked
to the system is the best option.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.820
Abraham: However, as we said earlier,
by adding security at the beginning

00:03:53.829 --> 00:03:57.850
in the design stage all these issues
can be factored into the process to

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:00.100
make a system as secure as possible.

